“Stone Father,” she said, “open your ribs and let the rain walk.”

The mountain groaned. “Water without wisdom breaks what it loves.”

“Then let it learn the village,” Marel answered. She cut away her shadow and braided it into the cord, marking doorways, gardens, cradles, and graves.

The mountain split. Water poured out, but the cord drew it gently downhill. It bent around homes, soaked the fields, and gathered itself into the first river.

Marel returned beneath noonlight unshadowed. Since then, every river carries one dark braid beneath its brightness, remembering mercy’s road always.
