Myth: In the elder dawn, water lived behind a door of salt beneath the mountains, dreaming of clouds, reeds, and the mouths of deer. The villages above knew only dust. Their jars rang empty, and the children drew rivers in ash to remember curves they had never seen.

Among them was Sera, a maker of sandals, who knew every path by the shape it left on leather. When the drought became a hard law, she asked the people for signs of kindness. A hunter spared a doe. A proud man carried water for his rival’s mother. A child planted one seed though the field was dead. Sera pressed these mercies into a pair of blue sandals and climbed to the mountain’s sealed gate.

“Open,” she begged, “and let water return.”

The gate answered, “Water has swift feet. It may trample the world.”

“Then let it wear these,” Sera said. She unlaced her shadow and stitched it through the soles, teaching them the roads around cradles, graves, hearths, and wheat.

The salt door opened. Water stepped out carefully, first as a spring, then as a river, walking where mercy guided.

Sera came home shadowless. Since then, rivers wear dark sandals under their shining feet, remembering to hurry gently toward thirsty life.
