“Open,” she begged, “and let water return.”

The gate answered, “Water has swift feet. It may trample the world.”

“Then let it wear these,” Sera said. She unlaced her shadow and stitched it through the soles, teaching them the roads around cradles, graves, hearths, and wheat.

The salt door opened. Water stepped out carefully, first as a spring, then as a river, walking where mercy guided.

Sera came home shadowless. Since then, rivers wear dark sandals under their shining feet, remembering to hurry gently toward thirsty life.

