Nima, who carved cradles from fallen branches, believed every kindness left a grain in the world. She gathered them: a sister sharing bread, a shepherd carrying a lame lamb, two rivals planting shade beside the road. From cedar shavings and these invisible grains, she made a little nest.

At moonrise she knelt among the roots. “Old Cedar, let the bird wake.”

The tree groaned. “Water has wings. If it flies frightened, it will drown valleys and tear houses from their names.”

